#text(lang: "en")[
  In an age of increasingly growing digital infrastructures, the reliability and stability of networks are of utmost importance. Manual network diagnostics and troubleshooting require a lot of time, are prone to errors, and often suffer from a lack of standardization in documentation.

  This thesis deals with the implementation of “DiagNet”, a modular and web-based application for automated network diagnostics. The project, which was supported by CANCOM Austria AG through the provision of hardware, aims to simplify the verification of network states and the detection of anomalies in network environments. The result is a functioning open-source application under the GPLv3+ license.

  The technological core of the application is based on the Python web framework Django and uses the Cisco pyATS ecosystem to interact programmatically with network devices. This approach enables the abstraction of manufacturer-specific command lines into structured test cases. Key features include an engine for dynamic test generation, status monitoring, and a user-friendly dashboard for visualizing diagnostic results.
]
